From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 08:53:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wintrust: Check the Authenticode policy for certificates
 signed with SHA-256.

The disallowed check read the certificate's signature hash into twenty
bytes, which a SHA-256 hash does not fit, and its failure skipped the
chain policy as well and left no error: a signer whose certificate is
signed with SHA-256, as all of today's are, passed whatever its chain,
expired, untrusted or self-signed.  And the hash went to
CertFindCertificateInStore as bytes, not the blob it takes.  Windows 11
says CERT_E_EXPIRED for VSTOInstaller.exe with its time stamp cut out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/dlls/wintrust/softpub.c b/dlls/wintrust/softpub.c
index 6279ca961d1..e0f92e8ee32 100644
--- a/dlls/wintrust/softpub.c
+++ b/dlls/wintrust/softpub.c
@@ -1419,7 +1419,10 @@ HRESULT WINAPI SoftpubAuthenticode(CRYPT_PROVIDER_DATA *data)
         ret = TRUE;
         for (i = 0; ret && i < data->csSigners; i++)
         {
-            BYTE hash[20];
+            /* the hash of what the certificate's signature signs, in the
+             * signature's algorithm: SHA-256 and longer as well as SHA-1
+             */
+            BYTE hash[64];
             DWORD size = sizeof(hash);
 
             /* First make sure cert isn't disallowed */
@@ -1435,9 +1438,10 @@ HRESULT WINAPI SoftpubAuthenticode(CRYPT_PROVIDER_DATA *data)
 
                 if (disallowed)
                 {
+                    CRYPT_HASH_BLOB blob = { size, hash };
                     PCCERT_CONTEXT found = CertFindCertificateInStore(
                      disallowed, X509_ASN_ENCODING, 0, CERT_FIND_SIGNATURE_HASH,
-                     hash, NULL);
+                     &blob, NULL);
 
                     if (found)
                     {
